Description_Detailed:
The entire “real” program boiled down to this:

1. It reads a single integer N from standard input.  
2. If N == 1 it prints one fixed message; otherwise it prints the other fixed message.  

All of the rest – the giant `Foster` and `InputReader` classes, the `sort(long[])` helper, the `Point` class, the dozens of dummy branches and encrypted‐string loaders – are never invoked by `main()`’s simple two‐way conditional. In effect, it’s just:

    int n = readInt();
    if (n == 1) 
        System.out.println(MSG1);
    else 
        System.out.println(MSG0);

where MSG0 and MSG1 are the two obfuscated/encrypted literals.
